          <div class="p-3.5 rounded-2xl bg-amber-50/80 border border-amber-200 text-xs text-amber-950">
            <strong>2. Reading: Farming changes the world</strong><br>
            For a very long time, people did not have farms. They hunted wild animals and gathered fruit, nuts and seeds from wild plants. They moved from place to place to find food. Then, <strong>about 11,000 years ago</strong>, something very important happened: people began to grow their own food. This was the start of the <strong>Neolithic period</strong>. How did farming change the way people lived? What did they eat, and what work did they do?
          </div>

          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-tealDeep mb-1">A. Where and when were the first farmers?</h4>
            <p>The first farmers lived in <strong>Mesopotamia</strong>, a land between two big rivers, the Tigris and the Euphrates. Today, this area is part of <strong>Iraq and Syria</strong>. The land near the rivers was <strong>flat and fertile, and there was a lot of water</strong>, so it was a good place to grow crops.</p>
            <p class="mt-2">The first farmers planted grain crops such as <strong>wheat and barley</strong>. They also grew <strong>peas, lentils and flax</strong>. They ate the peas and lentils, and they used flax to make cloth for their clothes.</p>
            <p class="mt-2">At the same time, people began to domesticate animals. Before, they hunted wild sheep, goats, cows and pigs. Now they kept these animals on their farms (<strong>sheep, goats, cows and pigs</strong>), so they had meat and milk all year. However, farmers did not stop hunting completely. They still <strong>hunted wild animals, caught fish and collected fruit and nuts</strong>.</p>
            <p class="mt-2 text-xs bg-linen p-2.5 rounded-xl border border-stone-200">
              Farming spread very slowly from Mesopotamia to other places. It reached the <strong>south of Europe about 2,000 years later</strong>.<br>
              <strong class="text-tealDeep">Note:</strong> <em>BCE = Before the Common Era. We count BCE years backwards, so 9000 BCE is older than 3000 BCE.</em>
            </p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-tealDeep mb-1">B. Changing the land</h4>
            <p>Farmers changed the land around them. They <strong>cut down trees</strong> and <strong>cleared the land</strong> to make fields for their crops. Their animals ate the wild plants and grass. To bring water to the fields, farmers <strong>dug long water channels</strong> from the river. Some farmers <strong>built a dam</strong> across a river to keep and store water. In places with hills, farmers <strong>made terraces</strong>. Terraces are flat steps of land on the side of a hill, so the rain does not wash the soil away. Farmers in Vietnam still grow rice on terraces today.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-tealDeep mb-1">C. Changing food</h4>
            <p>Because people grew their own grain, they had more food. Farmers ground wheat and barley between two stones to <strong>make flour</strong>, and then they <strong>made bread and porridge</strong>. Their animals gave them <strong>meat and milk</strong>. Food was now easier to find, so families did not need to move any more. They built houses and <strong>lived together in villages</strong>.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-tealDeep mb-1">D. New tools and technology</h4>
            <p>Farming led to new inventions. Later, farmers <strong>invented the plough</strong>, a tool that breaks up the soil so seeds can grow. Strong animals, such as cows, pulled the plough, so farmers could prepare a big field quickly. Some farmers in Vietnam still use <strong>buffaloes</strong> to plough their rice fields.</p>
            <p class="mt-2">Stone tools also got better. People <strong>polished their stone axes and blades</strong>, so they were smooth and sharp and cut better. People also learned to <strong>make pottery</strong>. They shaped wet clay into pots and bowls, and then they baked them in a fire to make them hard. They used the pots to store grain, water and milk (clay pots made in about <strong>3200 BCE</strong>). Farmers also <strong>made textiles</strong>: linen from flax, and wool from sheep and goats to make warm clothes.</p>
          </div>

          <div>
            <h4 class="font-display font-bold text-base text-tealDeep mb-1">E. New jobs</h4>
            <p>In a farming village, there were many different jobs. Some people <strong>looked after the animals</strong>, and others <strong>worked in the fields</strong>. Some people became very good at making pots, so <strong>pottery</strong> became their special job. Sometimes a village grew more food than it needed. People <strong>traded</strong> the extra food with other villages for things they did not have, such as tools, special stones or animals.</p>
          </div>
